d. (5,2) covered by x+y=7. (5,3) sum=8 >7, not needed. (5,4) sum=9 >7.\n\n(6,1) covered by x+y=7. (6,2) sum=8 >7.\n\nSo uncovered points after using those 4 non-sunny lines: (4,1), (4,2), (5,1). That's three points. We have two sunny lines left to cover them. Can we cover these three points with two sunny lines? Let's see.\n\nPoints: (4,1), (4,2), (5,1). We need to cover them with two lines, each not parallel to axes or slope -1.\n\nWe could try to use a sunny line covering (4,1) and (5,1). That's horizontal line y=1, slope 0, not allowed. So can't.\n\nSunny line covering (4,1) and (4,2) is vertical line x=4, not allowed.\n\nSunny line covering (4,2) and (5,1) slope = (1-2)/(5-4) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus any line covering any two of these three points is either vertical, horizontal, or slope -1, all non-sunny. So we cannot cover any pair of these points with a sunny line. So we need at least three lines to cover them if we restrict to sunny lines. But we have only two sunny lines. However, we could use a non-sunny line for one of them, but we have used all four non-sunny lines already. But we could replace one of the non-sunny lines with a different one to free up a non-sunny line for covering one of these points. For instance, we could replace x=3 with a sunny line covering some points in column 3, freeing up a non-sunny line to cover (4,1) or (5,1). But we have only two sunny lines total, so we need to allocate them wisely.\n\nLet's try a different approach: Use non-sunny lines: vertical lines x=1, x=2, x=3, x=4 (four non-sunny lines). That covers all points in columns 1-4. Then uncovered points: column 5: (5,1),(5,2); column 6: (6,1). That's three points. We have two sunny lines to cover them. As before, we cannot cover any pair of these points with a sunny line. So we cannot cover them with two sunny lines. So that fails.\n\nMaybe we can use non-sunny lines: vertical lines x=1, x=2, x=3, and a horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny). That covers many points: x=1 cover